---
import { C, FONT, markerDefs } from "../infographicTokens";

const defs = markerDefs("tgd");

/**
 * Why the 90 °C base matters: 12 AWG THHN in a 40 °C ambient with six
 * current-carrying conductors survives a 20 A breaker only because derating starts
 * from 30 A. Starting from the 75 °C figure of 25 A would land at 18.2 A and fail.
 */
const rawSteps = [
  { label: "90 °C base", value: "30.0 A", note: "Table 310.16, THHN", accent: C.orange, bg: C.orangeLight },
  { label: "× 0.91 ambient", value: "27.3 A", note: "40 °C, 310.15(B)(1)", accent: C.blue, bg: C.blueLight },
  { label: "× 0.80 bundling", value: "21.8 A", note: "6 conductors, 310.15(C)(1)", accent: C.blue, bg: C.blueLight },
  { label: "75 °C ceiling", value: "21.8 A", note: "under 25 A, so it stands", accent: C.primary, bg: C.primaryLight },
];

// Precompute the connector flag in frontmatter — a bare comparison inside the
// template risks being read as an opening tag by the Astro compiler.
const steps = rawSteps.map((s, i) => ({ ...s, hasArrow: i !== rawSteps.length - 1 }));
---

<svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" viewBox="0 0 720 300" fill="none" aria-hidden="true">
  <title>Why the 90 degree base matters when derating 12 AWG</title>
  <desc>
    Derating 12 AWG THHN from its 90 degree base of 30 amps: multiply by 0.91 for a 40 degree Celsius
    ambient to get 27.3 amps, then by 0.80 for six current-carrying conductors to get 21.8 amps. That
    result is below the 25 amp termination ceiling so it stands, and because 21.8 exceeds 20 the circuit
    keeps its 20 amp breaker. Had derating started from the 75 degree figure of 25 amps, the result would
    have been 18.2 amps and the circuit would have failed.
  </desc>

  <defs set:html={defs} />

  <rect x="8" y="8" width="704" height="284" rx="12" fill={C.panelBg} stroke={C.border} />
  <text x="24" y="36" fill={C.dark} font-family={FONT} font-size="14" font-weight="700">Why you start derating at 30 A, not 25 A</text>
  <text x="24" y="54" fill={C.muted} font-family={FONT} font-size="11">12 AWG THHN · 40 °C ambient · 6 current-carrying conductors in one raceway</text>

  {steps.map((s, i) => {
    const x = 24 + i * 172;
    return (
      <g>
        <rect x={x} y="80" width="152" height="92" rx="10" fill={s.bg} stroke={s.accent} stroke-width="1.5" />
        <text x={x + 76} y="104" text-anchor="middle" fill={C.dark} font-family={FONT} font-size="10.5" font-weight="800">{s.label}</text>
        <text x={x + 76} y="136" text-anchor="middle" fill={s.accent} font-family={FONT} font-size="22" font-weight="800">{s.value}</text>
        <text x={x + 76} y="158" text-anchor="middle" fill={C.muted} font-family={FONT} font-size="9">{s.note}</text>
        {s.hasArrow && <path d={`M${x + 156} 126 L ${x + 168} 126`} stroke={C.border} stroke-width="1.6" marker-end="url(#tgd-arrow-muted)" />}
      </g>
    );
  })}

  <!-- Verdict row -->
  <rect x="24" y="192" width="324" height="56" rx="9" fill={C.greenLight} stroke={C.green} stroke-width="1.6" />
  <text x="40" y="214" fill={C.green} font-family={FONT} font-size="11.5" font-weight="800">21.8 A ≥ 20 A — 20 A breaker still good</text>
  <text x="40" y="234" fill={C.muted} font-family={FONT} font-size="9.5">Starting from the 90 °C column is what saved this circuit</text>

  <rect x="368" y="192" width="328" height="56" rx="9" fill={C.redLight} stroke={C.red} stroke-width="1.6" />
  <text x="384" y="214" fill={C.red} font-family={FONT} font-size="11.5" font-weight="800">Had you started at 25 A: 18.2 A — fails</text>
  <text x="384" y="234" fill={C.muted} font-family={FONT} font-size="9.5">Same install, wrong starting column, needless upsize</text>

  <text x="24" y="278" fill={C.muted} font-family={FONT} font-size="10">Push it to nine conductors and even the 90 °C base fails: 30 × 0.91 × 0.70 = 19.1 A, under 20 — then you do need 10 AWG.</text>
</svg>
